@genie:
What happened to commitment to one's profession? As an Autistic Support teacher, I hold a deep dedication to my students and their families. My perspective is by no means unique for special ed teachers. That trumps high salary for me and others like me any day.
As for pay and quality issues, you are wrong. People don't go into teaching for the money, nor do they generally leave for money. Teacher attrition is overwhelmingly influenced by school environment issues: supportive colleagues, safety and violence, the ability to be an effective educator . If you examine excellent school systems abroad, salary is generally similar to ours. However, entrance to an education school is considered a dream, only for the top tier of college graduates. the difference is in that teaching is culturally valued in those countries.

I just looked up transmission losses for the US grid at the EIA's website ( http://www.eia.doe.gov/ask/electricity_faqs.asp#electric_rates2). They put average losses (all) for the grid at 6%. That means to get 3 megawatts into the car you'd have to generate 3.18 megawatts of electricity. According to your other estimates that would equal 36*(11/3.16)= 124 mpg. That is burning coal, natural gas, wind power, hydro or whatever the mix at that local as delivered to your home. vs. diesel or bio-diesel or natural gas.
Ben: Life-cycle analysis is available. Several of the national labs have done them. Also, the materials in the batteries are recyclable to new batteries (li-ion, nickel-cadmium, or NMH) I don't think we can recycle our used gasoline.
@CBISCHOFF your estimate of $15,000 replacement cost for a new battery is higher than the cost of converting a prius to a plug-in hybrid with a brand-new lithium-ion battery pack. My parents Prius is at 150,000 miles with no decline in mileage yet. How many cars do you get to overhaul at 200,000  miles and get an increase in performance?

