@David Underwood, you are confusing "stupid" with "ignorant." Ignorance is lack of knowledge, whether willing or not. And yes, it may be stupid to be willfully ignorant, but that doesn't mean that stupid equals willful ignorance. There are stupid people with a wealth of knowledge.
Take the debate over climate change, for example. Without taking sides, I can say truthfully that scientists on both sides of the debate have a wealth of information and knowledge. They have devoted years of study to the topic. But one group or the other must be stupid--else all these well-informed, intelligent scientists would come to a consensus on the topic. (I will leave the direction of the consensus up to your judgement.)
However, since the end result may be the same, perhaps I am splitting hairs.

Thirty years ago Devo advanced the concept that we are actually "de-evolving", getting stupider, and I thought then, and still do, that there was something really going on that they were trying to point out.
I think different things are going on in different strata of the populations. Clearly, a lot of us are getting cleverer, and this is worldwide. However, in Britain and the States it seems obvious that there is a large, growing, number of people who simply have not learned how to think clearly. They have innate ability, but they have never been thought to think. If these people were plunked back into 1850 or 1930, they would not be able to survive.
I think the problem is threefold.
1) lazy, misguided parenting which doesn't pass on the attitudes, approaches, and skills needed to become a thinker;
2) a broken education system which has cheated an entire generation;
3) a culture which has thrown overboard traditional approaches which actually worked in favour of a "do your own thing", "anything goes" approach which has led to a shallow, superficial mass culture.

@Julemry,
The idea that much of the world lives on plants and grains alone has already been addressed by Tyler and Kent. Typically, this is not because "meat is a rare luxury for them." It is because, as Kent pointed out, meat is "a necessity in scarce supply." Tyler elaborated that the over-population is a large factor in this as well.
As a corollary to Tyler's point, the only reason that a lot of these societies are able to survive on plants (much less anything) is because of Norman Borlaug's (and others') teachings of methods to breed of high-yield and disease-resistant plants. Without that, upwards of a billion people (yes, literally, a billion) would be dead from starvation in west Asia. Borlaug and others helped push the rate of global food production higher than that of world population increases. The point is that not only do these societies subsist on plants and grains out of necessity, but, if not for clever botanists, not even vegetarianism would have prevented the famine that over-population would have sowed.

@James
Of course, there is a spectrum of sentiments on the ethics of killing organisms. Maybe you find the consumption of animals without developed nervous systems wrong. Well this can be taken this a step further: some people think snuffing out plant life is cruel too. After all, studies on tomato plants have shown that, while they don't have nervous systems, they do have an equivalent system of messaging. They send electrical signals when an invading organism breaches their body or when there is cellular damage. Plants are organisms too!
(By the way, it is a great stretch to say that oysters feel pain. They have two nerve ganglia and do react to irritants, but, because they do not have a brains, the suggestion that they "feel" pain is extremely far-fetched. What they have is the ability to register a stimulus, not cognitively, not as a feeling, simply a register that a stimulus occured and the biological reaction to that stimulus. In the case of oysters, the stimulus might be a piece of grit inside the shell, and the reaction would be the formation of a pearl around that piece of grit.)

